## Proof

Let $(\Omega, Q, P)$ be the underlying probability space. Define the limsup (infinitely-often) events:

$$A_{\text{i.o.}} = \limsup_{k\to\infty} A_k = \bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty}\bigcup_{k=n}^{\infty} A_k, \qquad (B^c)_{\text{i.o.}} = \limsup_{k\to\infty} B_k^c = \bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty}\bigcup_{k=n}^{\infty} B_k^c.$$

By hypothesis,
$$P(A_{\text{i.o.}}) = 1, \qquad P\!\big((B^c)_{\text{i.o.}}\big) = 0.$$

Let $B_{\text{a.e.}} := \big((B^c)_{\text{i.o.}}\big)^c = \bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty}\bigcap_{k=n}^{\infty} B_k$ be the event that $B_k$ occurs **eventually always** (i.e., for all sufficiently large $k$). Since $P((B^c)_{\text{i.o.}}) = 0$, we have

$$P(B_{\text{a.e.}}) = 1.$$

Now consider the event $C := A_{\text{i.o.}} \cap B_{\text{a.e.}}$. Both factors have probability $1$, so

$$P(C) = 1.$$

**Claim.** $C \subseteq \limsup_{k\to\infty}(A_k \cap B_k)$.

*Proof of claim.* Fix $\omega \in C$. Since $\omega \in B_{\text{a.e.}}$, there exists $N(\omega) \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $\omega \in B_k$ for every $k \geq N(\omega)$. Since $\omega \in A_{\text{i.o.}}$, the set $S(\omega) := \{k : \omega \in A_k\}$ is infinite. In particular, $S(\omega) \cap \{k : k \geq N(\omega)\}$ is infinite (removing finitely many indices from an infinite set leaves it infinite). For every $k$ in this intersection, $\omega \in A_k$ and $\omega \in B_k$, hence $\omega \in A_k \cap B_k$. Therefore $\omega \in A_k \cap B_k$ for infinitely many $k$, i.e., $\omega \in \limsup_{k\to\infty}(A_k \cap B_k)$. ∎

Since $C$ is contained in the limsup event and $P(C) = 1$,

$$P\!\Big(\limsup_{k\to\infty}(A_k \cap B_k)\Big) \geq P(C) = 1.$$

Probabilities are bounded above by $1$, so

$$\boxed{P\big(A_k \cap B_k \text{ infinitely often}\big) = 1.}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
